Here is a comprehensive, step-by-step lab guide designed for learners to understand how to transition from a standalone Puppet **manifest (`site.pp`)** to a modular, reusable **Puppet Module** using the professional Profile pattern.
This use case sets up a multi-tier web application stack on Ubuntu: **Apache2** acting as a reverse proxy, **OpenJDK 17**, **Apache Tomcat** running a sample `.war` application, and **PHP** processing web requests.

---

# Part 1: The Monolithic Puppet Manifest (`site.pp`)

Create or update your manifest file on your Puppet target server at `/etc/puppetlabs/code/environments/production/manifests/site.pp`:

1. Write the Monolithic site.pp Manifest:
Add the following resource declarations to define packages, configuration files, and services in logical order:
==================================================================
node default {
  # 1. Update package list & install base utilities
  exec { 'apt-update':
    command => '/usr/bin/apt-get update',
  }

  # 2. Install Apache2, PHP, and Java OpenJDK 17
  package { ['apache2', 'libapache2-mod-php', 'php', 'openjdk-17-jdk']:
    ensure => installed,
    require => Exec['apt-update'],
  }

  # 3. Enable Apache Proxy Modules for Reverse Proxy
  exec { 'enable-proxy-modules':
    command => '/usr/sbin/a2enmod proxy proxy_http headers',
    creates => /etc/apache2/mods-enabled/proxy.load,
    require => Package['apache2'],
    notify  => Service['apache2'],
  }

  # 4. Configure Apache Virtual Host as Reverse Proxy to Tomcat (Port 8080)
  file { '/etc/apache2/sites-available/tomcat-proxy.conf':
    ensure  => file,
    content => @(EOT),
      <VirtualHost *:80>
          ServerName localhost
          DocumentRoot /var/www/html

          # Proxy requests to Tomcat backend
          ProxyPass /app http://localhost:8080/sample/
          ProxyPassReverse /app http://localhost:8080/sample/

          ErrorLog ${APACHE_LOG_DIR}/proxy_error.log
          CustomLog ${APACHE_LOG_DIR}/proxy_access.log combined
      </VirtualHost>
      EOT
    require => Package['apache2'],
    notify  => Service['apache2'],
  }

  exec { 'enable-vhost':
    command => '/usr/sbin/a2ensite tomcat-proxy.conf && /usr/sbin/a2dissite 000-default.conf',
    creates => /etc/apache2/sites-enabled/tomcat-proxy.conf,
    require => File['/etc/apache2/sites-available/tomcat-proxy.conf'],
    notify  => Service['apache2'],
  }

  # 5. Manage Apache Service
  service { 'apache2':
    ensure     => running,
    enable     => true,
    hasrestart => true,
  }

  # 6. Install and Manage Tomcat Server
  package { 'tomcat9':
    ensure  => installed,
    require => Package['openjdk-17-jdk'],
  }

  service { 'tomcat9':
    ensure     => running,
    enable     => true,
    require    => Package['tomcat9'],
  }
}

==================================================================

Verification:* Run `sudo puppet parser validate /etc/puppetlabs/code/environments/production/manifests/site.pp` to check syntax correctness.


2. Apply the Manifest Locally:
Test your manifest by applying it directly on the server:

sudo puppet apply /etc/puppetlabs/code/environments/production/manifests/site.pp --noop

*Verification:* Run `curl http://localhost` and `sudo systemctl status apache2 tomcat9` to confirm both services are active.


